 :cli/fp-1 "This CLI IS the interface to a LIVE Sutra-OS backend — to learn what exists or what an app does, run a verb; don't grep src/ or read .ent files."
 :cli/fp-2 "Every verb authenticates ITSELF as admin. NEVER write sutra.wire.api/sutra.wire.transport/login/curl code — if a read looks missing, it's a verb you haven't found (`guide`), not an HTTP call to hand-roll."
 :cli/fp-3 "Start from your QUESTION, not a verb — and pick the ALTITUDE. `orient` verbs answer whole questions: what exists/is-live → overview/apps/app-data/projects; what happened → runs/run/jobs/health/cost. Drop to the low-level `inspect` primitives (describe/query/links/around/peek/source-*) only to slice ONE type/link/source — and NEVER shell-loop a verb or hand-assemble across links/RIDs what an `orient` verb already answers in one call."
 :cli/fp-4 "Read before you write, and scope every read: overview → describe/query → app/app-data. Use query --where/--show/--order and app-data --type/--limit; `--fields a,b.c` narrows ANY verb's --json to just those result paths (e.g. `overview --fields by-type`) — never dump a whole type into context. Follow LINKS with `query <type> --around side1,side2` — each hop names a link SIDE by its API name, which carries the direction (no direction flag) — (the far side becomes the set, so --where scopes the start and --limit bounds the result) instead of running one query per hop and joining by hand; `query <type> --explain` prints the PLAN — whether the read is bounded and filtered at all — without paying for it."
 :cli/fp-5 "Prod (whatever `.sutra-site.edn` names) is the DEFAULT target — a bare `sutra <verb>` operates prod. For local dev, `. bin/ports.sh` first (or pass `--base http://localhost:<port>`)."
 :cli/fp-6 "SCHEMA GOES THROUGH THE CLI, GIT CARRIES ONLY PYTHON. Every `.ent` text — ontology or code repository — is fetched with `sutra --branch <branch> projection pull ontologie <text>|--project <p> --to <absolute-dir>` (a checkout is the texts one pull names, not a folder), edited, checked with `projection push <absolute-path-of-text> --preview` and staged on the branch with `projection push <absolute-path-of-text>`; never `git push`, never `sutra change`. `sutra <file>.ent` installs nothing. Moving a resource into another text is cut and paste: it changes only where the projection holds it and lands at once."
 :cli/widget-refs "References in a layout:\n  {:item k}  field of the current row of a :loop, else of the object view's object\n  {:row k}   field of the clicked row, in effects only (:on-select, :on-row-click)\n  {:var h}   the variable itself, writable: :sel, the :var of a {:do :set}\n  {:val h}   the value of a variable or derived value, read-only\n  {:expr e}  a computed value; resolved BEFORE {:item}, so it does not see the row\nA condition may mix {:item} and {:var}: [:= {:item :id} {:var :selection}]."
 :cli/fp-7 "`main` IS OPEN: `projection push` without `--branch` lands at once, and so does a `git push` of Python to `main`. A branch is for risky work and for `slice`: `branch <branch>` → `projection push` → `commit` → `propose --source <branch> --target main` → `proposal <id>` (what it `requires`) → `approve <id>` only where a task requires it → `merge-proposal <id>`. Name the branch LEADING on EVERY call (`--branch <branch>`), or the CLI silently answers from `main`; without a terminal, a writing verb needs `--yes`. git stays only for `transforms/*.py` in `clone <project>/<repo>` — and its push needs the branch's open proposal first."
